import{_ as t,o as s,c as o,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.D66Xq0Kv.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Your first mission","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/first-mission.md","filePath":"guide/first-mission.md"}'),n={name:"guide/first-mission.md"};function a(r,e,h,c,d,l){return s(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="your-first-mission" tabindex="-1">Your first mission <a class="header-anchor" href="#your-first-mission" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Your first mission&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The first time you open the console after installing Orbit, Ted has a mission ready. It opens with a two-minute briefing in four short chapters and then lists the steps that turn a fresh install into a cloud you can trust. You can skip anything, close it at any point and pick it up later from the card on the overview.</p><h2 id="briefing" tabindex="-1">The briefing <a class="header-anchor" href="#briefing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The briefing {#briefing}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Four chapters, each a few seconds long: your servers become one cloud, push code and it ships, your data is safe, and Ted watches with you. The chapters advance on their own and pause while your mouse or keyboard is on them. Use the arrow keys or the chapter dots to move around, or <strong>Skip the briefing</strong> to go straight to the steps. With reduced motion on, nothing moves and nothing advances by itself.</p><h2 id="steps" tabindex="-1">The steps <a class="header-anchor" href="#steps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The steps {#steps}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each step says in one line why it matters and can be settled in three ways: done right there, done somewhere else in the console and checked automatically, or skipped for now.</p><ol><li><strong>Secure your account.</strong> Set up the second factor and save the recovery codes, in the mission itself.</li><li><strong>Set Orbit&#39;s clock.</strong> Choose the <a href="./time-zone">time zone</a> schedules, notifications and the status page follow.</li><li><strong>Choose where alerts go.</strong> Add an email, Slack or Discord <a href="./notifications">channel</a> without leaving the mission.</li><li><strong>Ship your first project.</strong> Opens the project creation; the step checks itself once an application has deployed.</li><li><strong>Give it your domain.</strong> Opens the <a href="./dns">DNS page</a>; the step checks itself once a custom domain is verified.</li><li><strong>Keep your data safe.</strong> Opens Backups to add an S3 storage; the step checks itself once one is ready.</li><li><strong>Add a second server.</strong> Opens the server setup; the step checks itself once a second server is ready.</li></ol><p>Members invited to a workspace get the first two steps only. Steps already set up before the mission opens are checked on their own, so nobody is asked twice.</p><h2 id="coming-back" tabindex="-1">Coming back <a class="header-anchor" href="#coming-back" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Coming back {#coming-back}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The mission is kept with your account, so it follows you to any browser. Until it is finished, the overview shows <strong>Mission control</strong> with the next step. Afterwards, <strong>Settings → General → The mission handbook</strong> replays the briefing and shows the mission again; the command palette has <strong>Replay the onboarding</strong> too. Replaying never undoes anything you set up.</p><h2 id="report" tabindex="-1">For administrators <a class="header-anchor" href="#report" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;For administrators {#report}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The handbook card also shows how far the people using this Orbit got: how many started, how many finished, and which steps were done or skipped. The numbers come from the users&#39; own preferences on this server; nothing is sent anywhere.</p>',12)])])}const f=t(n,[["render",a]]);export{u as __pageData,f as default};
